# Instrument — read before any UI work

**The system is the token layer at the top of `index.html`** (`:root`, ~line 20 onward).
It is heavily self-documenting: the comments there explain *why* each value is what it is,
and **they are the authority**. Read them before editing any screen.

`design-system/design-system.md` is the dated written record of how the system got here.
Where it and `index.html` disagree, **the file wins** and the doc gets a new dated block.

**Superseded (2026-08-14):** the gold/teal/navy system, its `--k-*` tokens in
`design-system/design-tokens.css`, and its "no blue accents" / "teal for positive" rulings
are **withdrawn**. They described `apps/review/`, which is no longer the main file.

## Non-negotiable

1. **The ramp is the palette.** Twelve neutral steps (`--n-0` … `--n-900`) carry the whole
   instrument. If a new hue seems necessary, the answer is a different ramp step.
2. **ONE accent: cobalt `--accent` `#2F62D4`**, and it carries interaction. `--accent-2`
   (teal-slate) is admitted for the "incurred/actual" measure alone. Indigo `--ai` is
   reserved for AI surfaces (Ask Korvyn, Insight, generated recommendations) and must
   never become a second UI accent — it is a *marker*, never a fill.
3. **Red / amber / green are STATE ONLY** — never decoration, never a series colour.
   Series step through the ramp into the accent (`--series-1` … `--series-4`).
4. **Two planes, named by role, not lightness:** content (the ramp) and chrome
   (`--chrome-*`). Chrome is a true neutral, never blue-tinted; header and sidebar share
   one base and separate by a hairline, never by lightness. Gated by
   `tools/check_chrome_themes.mjs`.
   - **Amended 2026-08-26 (owner):** the **left rail moved to the content plane** — a
     light rail in light mode (cobalt active state), dark in dark mode — while the
     **ribbon stays dark chrome**. Header and rail now differ by plane by intent. The
     light treatment is `.rail`-scoped overrides onto content tokens; the shared
     `.railrole` base (also used by the dark ribbon nav) is untouched.
5. **Separation is lightness and hairlines, never shadow.** Shadow is reserved for true
   overlays — popovers, menus, modals, drawers *over* content. Cards, panels, tables,
   strips and docked side panels are flat.
6. **Six type sizes and only these six** (`--fs-label` … `--fs-page`). `--fs-micro` is for
   counts and badge numerals that carry no sentence, **never prose**. `--fs-hero` has
   exactly one caller. A hard-coded `px` font-size is a seventh size — do not add one.
7. **TWO font weights: 400 and 500.** `--fw-label` is already 500, so a section label
   cannot be "made bolder" — hierarchy comes from size, tracking, ink and hairlines.
   Spacing is the 4px scale (`--s-1` … `--s-12`).
8. **Severity is a 3px left border and nothing else** — never a dot, never a pill, never
   both. Provenance dots are the one element allowed semantic colour at rest, and only
   when stale or unreachable, so a healthy screen stays monochrome.
9. On any data table, colour **at most two columns** — the primary variance and its
   direction. Gray all supporting columns (Organic, FX, CTA, NCI).
10. Subtotal rows outrank detail rows. Never equal weight.
11. **One card per screen REGION**, not per table or row. Tables use row hairlines, never
    card borders or per-cell borders. **No box-in-box.**
12. **Badges mean "action required from the user,"** not "count of items." Inventory
    counts render as quiet gray text.
13. **One filled primary button per screen.** All others outline.
14. All money, dates, IDs, deltas: tabular-nums, right-aligned, `--num` (Inter tabular)
    for figures and `--mono` for identifiers. Chrome uses Inter. Never blend them.
15. Every table and form ships with **empty, loading, error, hover and visible
    keyboard-focus states** — not just the happy path.

## Shared primitives — extend these, never restyle buttons (2026-08-28)

**Tabs — `.ktabs` / `.ktab`.** The one tab language. 32px (`--h-tab`), `0 12px`,
`--fs-table`, 2px underline, no pill, no fill, no shadow, no radius; the strip scrolls
before it wraps. **Two levels, one vocabulary:** level 1 is where you ARE (primary view
navigation) and takes the accent; `.ktabs.lvl2` is identical geometry with an **ink**
underline, for a secondary/command row. Two accent underlines on one screen is competing
navigation — one accent, and the hierarchy reads without a second colour, size or shape.
Rows are keyboard-driven by one delegated handler: roving `tabindex`, ←/→/Home/End.

**Dropdowns — `.pop`.** One anchored menu for every selector.
- Two fixed widths: 300px, or 340px `.wide` for a menu carrying a second column of
  figures or a form. Never narrower than the control it opens from.
- One inset (`--pop-x`) for header, search, rows and footer, so a section label and its
  rows start on the same pixel. Section separators are `.pop-h.sec` / `.pop-l.sec`,
  never an inline `border-top`.
- `--shadow-md`, not `--shadow-lg` — an anchored menu is not a modal (rule 5 still
  reserves shadow for true overlays; this is the restrained end of that allowance).
- Anchoring is live: re-placed on scroll and resize, right-aligns to the trigger rather
  than sliding along the viewport, closes when the trigger leaves the screen.
- **Single-select is a tick + a selected ROW (`--accent-bg`); multi-select is a checkbox
  and a neutral row.** A filling box reads as "one of several" — never draw one where
  only one answer is possible. Twelve accent-filled rows is a loud selected state.
- Keyboard throughout: ↑/↓/Home/End/Enter/Space, Escape closes and returns focus to the
  trigger. Roles are assigned after paint, so a new menu is covered automatically.
- Long lists get a search.

**Amends rule 2 for filter fields:** the accent marks the **open** field, not the
filtered one. A narrowing filter is stated by its **value** — `--hint` at rest, `--ink`
at 500 against a stronger border once it narrows — and the guarantee that a filtered
view says so is carried by per-tab counts, not by colour. Filter rows never wrap: one
row, scrolling.

**Never redefine `--radius` in a subtree.** It rounds every control that asks for the
token and forces per-control pins back to 6px.

## Boxy — a ground carrying cards (2026-08-28, owner's direction)

**Supersedes "4b UNBOXED".** A screen is a light **ground**; each region standing on
it is its own white card with a real edge. The page container is not the card — it
has no background, no border, no radius. This honours rule 11 literally (one card per
region, not one card per screen) and keeps rule 5 (separation is lightness + a
hairline, **never shadow** — these cards are flat).

- **A card edge and an inner divider are not the same line.** Outer edge of a region →
  `--line2` (n-300). Dividers inside a card → `--line` (n-200). Against the darker
  page `--line` all but vanishes, and a card reads as white paint rather than an object.
- Light-mode `--bg` is `var(--n-100)`, not `var(--n-50)` — a card must be visibly
  lifted off its ground. **The dark block is unchanged**: the ramp inverts there, so
  `--n-50` is already the darkest step and is correctly the page.
- **Primary tabs are folder tabs** (`.ktabs.lvl1`): the selected tab is a white box
  whose bottom edge IS the panel's top edge. The strip carries no border-bottom of its
  own and sits above the panel in the stacking order. Secondary rows (`.lvl2`) keep the
  underline — a command row inside a panel is not opening a panel.
- Filter-row fields are `--h-field` (32px) on `--border-strong`: an input-shaped box you
  read a value out of, not a chip you press.

## The filter region — one object, everywhere (2026-08-28, owner's direction)

Every filter surface in the product is the same three-part card, on Flux (`#fxRoot`)
and on every other page (`#gfBar`):

```
⇶ FILTERS   2 active                              Clear all   ▸   ← .kfh, the toggle
Period  Scope ¹  Workflow  Variance  Basis │ Display  Saved Views  ← .ktabs.lvl2
[Fund  All ⌄]  [Region  All ⌄]  [Ownership  All ⌄]                ← one field row
```

- **The band is the toggle and starts folded.** It is a `role="button"` div, not a
  `<button>` — Clear all lives inside it and a nested button is invalid markup.
- **The count must survive the fold.** Collapsing may hide WHICH filters are set,
  never THAT any are. `N active` + Clear all stay on the band. Do not remove them.
- `.ktab-div` marks where the filters stop, so tabs that configure rather than
  filter are not claimed by the word *Filters*.
- **Fields drive existing controls; they never hold state.** Set the underlying
  `<select>.value` and dispatch `change` so the control's own handler runs. Read
  options off that control too — never restate them in a registry.
- **A field with nothing to choose does not render.** No dead controls.
- One menu: `.pop`. New surfaces declare filters as data and get a `g:<key>` menu
  with the shared keyboard, anchoring, width and selected-state.
- The field CSS is `:is(#fxRoot,#gfBar) .fxf` / `.fx-fields`. **Add a surface by
  adding it to that `:is()`**, never by copying the block.
- **ONE filter affordance per surface.** The band is the only way in — never add a
  page-level "Filters" button inside the content, and never a second "+ Filter" chip.
  Both regions fold whenever the page changes (`_lastFiltTab` in `paintTopbar`).
## One page template (2026-08-28, owner's direction)

Every view is the same three bands: **shell title row** (name + one context line) ->
**FILTERS band** (the options) -> **content** (driven by them).

- **The page states its name once.** `glHead()` drops its `h1` when it repeats the
  topbar's `VIEW_META` title (`glDupTitle()`). Keep the `h1` only when it names a
  RECORD rather than the view. Never add a second page header component - `fpHead()`
  delegates to `glHead()`.
- **A page must not render its own filter row.** Controls in the content are
  discovered (`.grp` holding a `<label>` + bound `<select>` inside `#view-<TAB>`) and
  surfaced as a **More filters** tab, then hidden with `[data-gf-moved]`. Discovery
  must NOT test visibility, or it stops finding the row it just hid.
- Discovered fields drive the original control (set `.value`, dispatch `change`) and
  read their options off it - never restate either.

- **A collapsed rail must not hide navigation.** With `.railkids` hidden, a group's
  children open as an anchored flyout — `pop(kind, el, /*side*/ true)` places the menu
  BESIDE its trigger. `toggleRailGroup()` returns false when it handled the click, so a
  collapsed rail neither navigates nor flips state nobody can see.
- The popover's outside-click dismissal listens on **`mousedown`**, not `click` — a
  synthetic `.click()` in a test will not trigger it.
- In `index.html`, `toggleFilters()` is also the file's **placeholder handler** for ~25
  unrelated buttons (Export, Columns, Actions, New request, Run Report…). Do not treat a
  `toggleFilters()` caller as a filter control without reading its label first.

**The UI face is a system stack**, led by `"Segoe UI Variable Text"` then
`-apple-system`. `--num` is `var(--sans)` so chrome and figures share one face
(rule 14); identifiers keep `--mono`. Inter is deliberately NOT in the stack. Keep the
file self-contained: no `@font-face`, no CDN. If you change the face, verify tabular
figures measure a 0px width spread across all ten digits before shipping it.

## Consolidation-scale screens (600+ entities)

16. The roll-up tree is a status instrument: every node shows its own completion
    (mini-bar + fraction) AND an exception count rolled up from everything beneath it.
17. Tables default to exceptions + material movements only ("5 of 214 lines"); full detail
    sits behind a filter chip.
18. Multi-currency / elim / NCI data lives behind column-set toggles (Local / Reporting /
    FX-CTA / Eliminations / NCI split), never one wide table.

## Docked panels — the inspect panel and the Korvyn Assistant (2026-08-29)

19. **A docked panel is a sheet of full-width BANDS, not a stack of floating cards.**
    Each section runs edge to edge and pads to `--s-4`, exactly as the header band
    does, so every word in the panel starts on the same pixel. Separation is a
    hairline between bands — lightness and a hairline, never shadow (rule 5). This
    supersedes "regions are the cards" for these panels; the statement page keeps its
    cards. On the full page the COLUMN is the sheet and the bands sit inside it.
20. **Nothing inside a band draws its own box.** Box-in-box is rule 11 verbatim, and
    it is what put the inspect panel's card title 15px from the edge and its own prose
    31px.
21. **Check alignment against the HEADER, not against sibling content.** A stack that
    is uniformly inset is uniformly symmetric and passes every card-to-card test while
    being visibly narrower than the chrome above it.
22. **A scrollbar takes layout width.** Chrome outside the scroller is therefore wider
    than bands inside it unless the gutter is reserved (`scrollbar-gutter:stable`) and
    taken as a right margin by the header, tabs and footer. Measure the width at
    runtime — it is a platform fact, not a token.
23. **Window controls are ONE primitive across every docked panel** — same target,
    glyph, colour, hover and cluster gap, on one shared rule. A new panel joins that
    rule; it never copies the values.
24. **`.ai` is a global component (the AI answer panel), not a modifier.** It carries
    `display:none` until `.ai.show`, so using it as a local class renders the element
    at 0×0. The colour token is `--ai`; the local modifier is `krv`.
25. **A band measures the same at every density and on every tab** — `padding:--s-4`,
    one bottom hairline, none on the last band in a column. The full page's solo tabs
    drew `20px 16px 16px` with a TOP border under the previous band's bottom one — two
    hairlines 20px apart — because `#fxRoot .rw-col.solo .rw-rg+.rw-rg` is one id and
    four classes and beat the page's card rule (one id, three). When you reset that
    separation, restore the card's own `padding-top`; zeroing it puts the title hard
    against the hairline.
26. **A card title is `--ink` at every density.** `.rw-card .rw-sec` declares it, and two
    later rules (`#fxRoot.aw …` `--hint`, `#fxRoot.aw:not(.dw-page) …` `--muted`) each
    won their tie on source order — so the same title rendered n-600 docked and n-500 on
    the page. A card states its own name in ink; the quiet tracked label is a BARE
    `.rw-sec` on a band and is what those rules are for.
27. **An empty state is still a band.** A part in `RW_NOCARD` (its normal output is its
    own cards) returns a bare node when there is nothing to show, and inherits padding
    from nothing — "Nothing said yet." printed 16px left of every other word in the panel.
28. **Where the reference uses colour and this system does not, the system wins.** The
    `apps/review` inspector colours driver amounts, file-type icons and every activity
    badge; rule 3 gives red/amber/green to STATE alone. So: Key drivers foot in plain
    ink and the +/− sign carries direction; a file's type is a monochrome tag, not a
    red PDF and a green spreadsheet; and an activity badge is coloured only for the two
    events that are states (approved, returned/reopened) — the glyph carries the kind.
    A healthy review must read monochrome, or the one amber disc stops meaning anything.
29. **Never assert a control that does not exist.** The reference's "Target sign-off ·
    4 days left" has no per-line target in this model, so the row states the real
    request due date under its own name and is ABSENT when nothing is due — never a
    placeholder date. Same discipline as the evidence table deriving Verified/Pending
    from the line's own sign-off.
30. **An audit trail records changes, not views.** A density toggle logged into a line's
    append-only record drove one line's History from 0 to 9 in a session and, matching
    `/return/`, badged every collapse amber as if the line had been sent back.
31. **THREE TEXT GREYS, AND `--faint` IS NOT ONE OF THEM.** `--ink-strong` / `--ink` /
    `--muted` / `--hint` are the foregrounds; `--faint` (n-400) is what the ramp's own
    comment says — "placeholder, disabled, trailing glyphs" — and reads 2.36:1 on white,
    2.09:1 on `--bg`, under the text floor AND the non-text floor. It carried 141 CSS
    rules and 9 JS literals as `color:`. The only survivors are one `::placeholder` and
    one `[disabled]`, the two exemptions WCAG grants. `--n-300` is a BORDER step and is
    not a foreground either (it was painting the sort caret at 1.43:1).
32. **Check a text colour against `--bg`, not against a white card.** `--hint` cleared AA
    at 4.80:1 on `--surface` and failed at 4.25:1 on `--bg`, so the same label passed
    inside a card and failed on the page. Light `--n-500` is `#656C7F` for this reason —
    worst 4.64:1 across all six content surfaces.
33. **Ink on a saturated fill must INVERT: use `--on-fill`.** `--on-accent`/`--on-neg` are
    white in both blocks, which is right in light mode and 2.19:1 in dark, where the
    semantics go light. `--on-fill` is white / `#141824`. It cannot be `var(--n-900)` —
    that step inverts too. Applies wherever a fill comes from DATA (a style attribute),
    because no CSS rule states that pairing.
34. **`node tools/check_text_contrast.mjs` gates all three of the above**, statically, off
    the tokens in `index.html`. The chrome gate does not cover the content plane and never
    did. When measuring contrast in the browser instead, inject
    `*{transition:none!important}` first — elements with a colour transition report
    mid-transition values right after a theme flip.
35. **`--fs-card` is 17px, and the jump to it is load-bearing.** A card resolves as one
    object through the step from its title to its body: 17/500 over 12/400 is 1.42×, up
    from 1.25× at 15px. The extra reach comes from SIZE, not a third weight — rule 7
    admits 400 and 500 and this does not spend a 600 to get there. Still six sizes
    (10/11/12/13/17/20); a value changed, not a size added. The token has 78 callers and
    is not only titles, so check what a change to it actually enlarges before shipping
    one.
36. **THREE WEIGHTS: 400 · 500 · 600 — this AMENDS rule 7.** `--fw-strong` (600) is spent
    on ONE thing, the title of a card or panel region. Not on figures (several `--fs-card`
    callers are amounts, and a heavy numeral is emphasis, a different claim). Two traps:
    font-weight INHERITS, so bolding a title row bolds the count, caret and controls
    beside the name — reset element children with `> *`; and the rule must sit at the
    FOOT of the sheet, because each selector ties with what it overrides and a tie breaks
    on source order (`.rw-sec` needs one id + four classes). `b`/`strong` are pinned to
    `--fw-strong` too: unstyled they fall through to the UA's `bolder` = 700, which was a
    fourth, undeclared weight on 543 elements.
37. **The quiet text scale is n-700 / n-600, not n-600 / n-500.** `--muted` is the app's
    most-used text colour and at 6.48:1 it was what made a screen of labels read soft;
    it is n-700 (10.00 / 8.84 on `--bg`) and `--hint` is n-600 (6.48 / 5.73). Four text
    levels, all clear of AA on every content surface in both modes. n-500's tuned value
    still matters — 85 rules name it directly instead of going through `--hint`.
38. **The type is EMBEDDED and the file is genuinely self-contained.** `--sans` leads with
    Inter and `--mono` with IBM Plex Mono, both base64 woff2 in the file; the Google Fonts
    `@import` that used to fetch Inter, Newsreader and IBM Plex Mono is gone, and `--serif`
    falls back to Georgia. Never re-add an `@import` or a `<link>` for type — embed it, the
    way these are embedded. Mono ships 400/500/600 only, matching the declared weights.
39. **Density lives in tokens — change it there.** `--pad-card` 16 · `--pad-cell` 0 12 ·
    `--pad-kpi` 12/16 · `--pad-toolbar` 8 · `--h-section` 32 · `--row-h` 44, all on the 4px
    `--s-*` scale. `--row-h` is the biggest single lever on how the product feels; dial it
    at the token, never in the rules that read it. The Flux statement derives from it
    (`calc(var(--row-h) - 4px)`) rather than hard-coding a height — it used to be the one
    table opting out, so a platform density change moved everything except the reference
    screen.
40. **A "platform-wide" change is not platform-wide until the CHROME moves too.** The
    ribbon, rail and close strip run on `--chrome-text*` from the theme definitions — only
    6 of 152 chrome rules touch `--muted`/`--hint` — so a content-token pass leaves the
    frame you see on every screen exactly as it was. Check both planes.
42. **A control SETS a height from a token; it does not fall out of padding.** Eight
    heights shipped (22–36px) because the button family derived its box from `padding:9px`
    plus a line-height. Three tokens now — `--h-chip` 24 · `--h-btn-sm` 28 · `--h-btn` 32,
    the last equal to `--h-tab` and `--h-field` — and controls take `height:var(--h-*)`
    with horizontal padding only. 92% of controls are on one; the strays are unclassed
    one-offs, not shared components.
43. **Line-height comes from `--lh-tight` / `--lh` / `--lh-relaxed` (1.2 / 1.5 / 1.62).**
    Sixteen literal ratios shipped while those tokens had four callers. `line-height:1`
    is exempt — that is glyph centring, not typography.
44. **`--fs-micro` is counts and numerals, never words.** The close strip had "CLOSE",
    "Day" and "Complete" at 10px; an uppercase tracked label is `--fs-label`.
46. **Spacing comes from `--s-1 … --s-12`, and `node tools/check_spacing_scale.mjs` gates
    it as a RATCHET.** The file carries 1,089 off-4px declarations that are not worth a
    sweep; the gate records them per value and fails if any count rises or a new value
    appears, so new code lands on the scale and old code converges. If a rule genuinely
    needs something between steps, derive it — `calc(var(--s-4) - 2px)` passes, a bare
    `15px` does not, and `padding:2px var(--s-2)` is caught as the half-tokenised value it
    is. Lower the baseline in the same commit as a fix; `--baseline` prints the line.
48. **A control with no `font-family` renders in the USER AGENT's font, not yours.** 62
    elements shipped in Arial because `font:inherit` was on some button rules and missing
    from others. One reset — `button,input,select,textarea,optgroup{font-family:inherit}` —
    and `code,kbd,samp,pre{font-family:var(--mono)}` for the same hole on the mono side.
    Never rely on per-rule `font:inherit` again.
49. **The scale is 10 · 11 · 12 · 13 · 17 · 20, plus `--fs-hero` 28.** `--fs-figure` is an
    ALIAS of `--fs-page`, not a size of its own — it must never sit BELOW `--fs-card`, which
    is what happened when the card title was raised and the figure was left behind, making
    every KPI value smaller than its own heading. SVG chart labels count: a presentation
    attribute cannot take `var()`, so use `style="font-size:var(--fs-*)"`, and cap a
    geometry-derived size with `min(calc(…), var(--fs-hero))`.
50. **Every numeral is `tabular-nums`, and `--num` IS the mono face** (rule 14 in full:
    money/dates/IDs/deltas are monospace; chrome is Inter; never blend). Both halves hold
    because **chrome does not use `--num`** — 0 of 152 chrome rules — so no exception has to
    be written. THE FACE GOES ON THE LEAF THAT HOLDS THE FIGURE — `td.num` yes, `th.num` no
    (a heading is a word), a named list of display-value selectors, never a wrapper. Do NOT
    key off `tabular-nums`: it is a container/header signal as often as a figure one, and
    because font-family inherits, that put mono on ~700 words (owner caught it — three panels,
    three faces). Traps: `.num` was defined by SEVEN rules and only one set a family, so
    flipping the token moves half a table; mono is 13.2% wider and `fxShed()` sheds columns
    silently, so measure first. **After any family change, grep the RENDERED DOM for words in
    the figure face — the CSS reasoning will lie to you.**
52. **The inspect panel is a 5-STEP hierarchy from the six tokens — one role each.** 20
    (`--fs-page`) = line name / subject, the LARGEST text (it was 13px, smaller than its own
    section titles — inverted). 17 (`--fs-card`) = section titles + header figures. 12
    (`--fs-table`) = all body. 11 (`--fs-label`) = UPPERCASE field labels. 10 (`--fs-micro`)
    = counts/captions. No 13px in the panel. Verify element-by-element in BOTH densities —
    page mode carries blocks the dock does not (e.g. `.rw-state.soft`).
53. **Every label/value card uses ONE field-row system.** Uppercase tracked `--muted` label
    in a fixed 100px column, LEFT-aligned `--ink` value (so values column-align and scan like
    a data sheet). Workflow & review and Financial source were three ways different
    (sentence/upper, 12/10px, right/left align) — they are byte-identical now. Do not
    right-align values or use sentence-case labels in these cards.
54. **One control shape inside a panel.** The inspector's segmented control inherited
    `--h-btn` (28px) beside `.sm` buttons at `--h-btn-sm` (26px), and a FILLED cobalt
    selected segment — a second filled primary against the footer's real one (rule 13).
    A chosen option takes the tinted state (`--accent-bg`/`--accent-ink`), as everywhere
    else in the app.

## Before calling any screen done

Run `node tools/check_chrome_themes.mjs` if chrome was touched, and report which of the
rules above the screen passes. **Never introduce a colour, font size, weight or spacing
value that is not already a token in `index.html`.**
